            <ArticleTitle>Analgesic principle from the bark of Careya arborea.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>698-701</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bioactivity guided isolation of the bark of Careya arborea afforded piperine--an alkaloid chemically known as 1-[5-(1,3-benzodioxol-5-yl)-1-oxo-2,4-pentadienyl]piperidine, which was found to possess significant central and peripheral analgesic activity. At oral doses of 10, 20 and 30 mg/kg body weight, piperine exhibited 41 (p &lt; 0.01), 45 (p &lt; 0.01) and 53% (p &lt; 0.001) inhibition of acetic acid induced writhing in mice respectively. At doses of 20 and 30 mg/kg body weight, the compound also showed 31.8 (p &lt; 0.05) and 52.4% (p &lt; 0.01) prolongation of tail flicking time of mice 30 min after the treatments determined by the radiant heat method.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
